  "# Codegen co-scientist, iteration 1

## Generate

Five candidates, read off one complete round of the emitted kernel (20
instructions, eight rounds):

| id | hypothesis | predicted |
|---|---|---|
| C1 | hoist the 64-bit reciprocal, identical in all eight rounds, into a callee-saved register | −7 instructions, −70 bytes, +3 setup |
| C2 | `mov rcx,r11` + `imul r8,rcx,M` → `imul r8,r11,M` | −8 instructions, −24 bytes |
| C3 | remove `mov rax,rdx` before the one-operand `imul` | −8 instructions |
| C4 | shorten the three-instruction sign correction | −16 instructions |
| C5 | remove the `push rax` / `push rdx` around the multiply | −16 instructions |

## Reflect

- **C3 rejected.** One-operand `imul` takes its dividend in RAX by hardware
  definition. The move is not a choice.
- **C4 rejected.** LLVM emits the same three instructions for the same
  correction on this target. A candidate that asks the backend to beat LLVM at
  a sequence LLVM also emits is asking for something the workload does not
  contain.
- **C5 rejected on evidence.** The saves look like waste and are not: the
  instruction after the restore is `movq %rax,%rcx`, so RAX is live across the
  division. This had already been proposed once on the strength of its shape
  and withdrawn after reading what came next.
- **C1 admitted**, with cost: a callee-saved register needs prologue and
  epilogue changes, so the win is smaller than the byte count suggests.
- **C2 admitted**, cheapest of the two: the same peephole shape as the
  immediate folding already landed, with the same single-use safety condition.

## Rank

C2 first, on predicted gain per unit of risk. C1 second.

## Measure

C2 implemented, tested and measured. **It removed one instruction, not eight.**

| | before | after |
|---|---:|---:|
| bytes | 571 | 568 |
| instructions | 165 | 164 |
| `movq` | 40 | 39 |
| median ns/call (21 rotated samples, Rosetta) | 14.93 | 14.92 |

The timing is identical, which is the correct reading of one instruction in 165
on a host whose noise floor is wider than that.

## Why the prediction was wrong

The eight `mov rcx,r11` instructions the candidate was counted from **are not
MIR instructions**. They are emitted inside `x86-quotient-constant`, which ends
by moving its result out of R11 into whatever `dst` the caller wants. No
peephole can see them, because they do not exist until after selection has run.

The fitness proxy — counting a repeated pattern in a disassembly — is sound
about what the machine executes and silent about **which pass owns it**. Every
candidate above was generated from that same disassembly, so the same blind
spot could have been under any of them.

C2 was landed anyway: it is correct, general, and demonstrably fires. On a
small function, where a copy is a larger share of the whole, `(+ n 7)` went from
17 bytes to 11. It was not landed as a speedup for this kernel, because it is
not one.

## Meta-review

- **Where ground truth exists, rank by it.** Four earlier candidates this
  session (narrow constants, immediate folding, stack-free division, `lea`)
  were each predicted from the same disassembly and each delivered; the fifth
  did not. A 4-of-5 static-prediction rate is worth knowing, and is only
  knowable because every candidate was measured rather than argued.
- **Add ownership to the fitness proxy.** A pattern in the disassembly should
  be traced to the pass that emits it before it is ranked. The cheap version of
  that check is a grep for the byte sequence in the emitters.
- **The refutation is the iteration's product.** Nothing else in this pass
  taught anything that was not already known.

## Next

C1 remains untested and is now the ranked candidate: eight identical ten-byte
loads of the same reciprocal, against the cost of reserving a callee-saved
register. Its ownership is known — `x86-quotient-constant` emits it — so the
mistake above cannot repeat in the same form.

The larger remaining item is not on this list at all: 39 register-to-register
moves that belong to allocation, which is what root ADR-2608179400 names as the
distance between amu and Cranelift."}]
